# `integ::lebesgue`

Integrates a function by partitioning its range instead of its domain, and returns a bracket the exact value must lie in.

## Signature

```cpp
integ::LebesgueResult integ::lebesgue(const integ::IntegrandFn &fn, double a, double b, const integ::LebesgueOptions &options = {});
```

## What It Does

Riemann-style rules such as [`quadra`](quadra.md) cut the **domain** into pieces. The Lebesgue construction cuts the **range**: it chooses levels `0 = t₀ < t₁ < … `, and the simple functions `s_lo = Σ tⱼ·1{tⱼ ≤ f < tⱼ₊₁}` and `s_hi = Σ tⱼ₊₁·1{tⱼ < f ≤ tⱼ₊₁}` squeeze `f` between them, so `∫ s_lo ≤ ∫ f ≤ ∫ s_hi`. The levels are chosen so that this bracket is narrower than `tolerance`, and the bracket is **returned** (`lower` and `upper`), so a value never arrives without the evidence for it. The interval is divided into graded cells whose number doubles until the value stops changing; the bracket certifies the range-partition error and the refinement difference reports the domain discretization.

It works on any integrand, including one with a singularity at an end point: set `grading > 1` to concentrate cells near `a` (the integrand `1/√x` on `(0, 1]`, which a uniform rule resolves badly). A non-negative and a negative part are bracketed separately. The integrand may return a batch, `[..., n]`, with the node axis last. This is an eager numerical evaluator; the analytic Lebesgue measures are in the calculus operators.

## Parameters

`const integ::IntegrandFn &fn`
: `Series -> Series`, the integrand at a `Series` of cell midpoints.

`double a, double b`
: A finite interval, `a < b`.

`const integ::LebesgueOptions &options`
: Cells, refinements, grading, tolerance.

## Returns

A [`LebesgueResult`](LebesgueResult.md).

## Errors

- A missing integrand, an infinite or empty interval, or a non-positive tolerance, grading or fewer than two cells throw.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

A bracket around `∫₀¹ x² dx = 1/3`. The lower and upper simple-function integrals enclose the value, and the estimate is half the bracket plus the refinement change.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace integ = pluss::subsp::math::integ;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(9));
  const integ::LebesgueResult r = integ::lebesgue([](const Series &t) { return t.square(); }, 0.0, 1.0);
  out.writeln("lower =", r.lower.item<double>(), ", value =", r.value.item<double>(), ", upper =", r.upper.item<double>());
  out.writeln("error estimate =", r.error_estimate, ", refinements =", r.refinements, ", converged =", r.converged, ", monotone =", r.monotone);
}
```

### Example 2

An endpoint singularity. `∫₀¹ x^(−1/2) dx = 2`; the cells are graded toward 0, where the integrand is unbounded.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace integ = pluss::subsp::math::integ;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  integ::LebesgueOptions options;
  options.grading = 3.0;
  options.tolerance = 1e-6;
  options.max_refinements = 12;
  const integ::LebesgueResult r = integ::lebesgue([](const Series &t) { return t.pow(-0.5); }, 0.0, 1.0, options);
  out.writeln("value =", r.value.item<double>(), ", exact = 2, converged =", r.converged);
}
```

### Example 3

A function that changes sign. `∫₋₁¹ x³ dx = 0`: the positive and negative parts are bracketed separately and cancel.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace integ = pluss::subsp::math::integ;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(9));
  const integ::LebesgueResult r = integ::lebesgue([](const Series &t) { return t.pow(3.0); }, -1.0, 1.0);
  out.writeln("value =", r.value.item<double>(), ", lower =", r.lower.item<double>(), ", upper =", r.upper.item<double>());
}
```

